WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:07.220
Can a cell's death be made to run in a test tube?

2
00:00:08.180 --> 00:00:12.820
In 1996, Xiaodong Wang's laboratory made it happen.

3
00:00:13.600 --> 00:00:16.660
They took the soluble contents of HeLa cells.

4
00:00:17.280 --> 00:00:20.220
They added the nucleotide dATP.

5
00:00:20.760 --> 00:00:23.040
And caspase-3 switched on.

6
00:00:23.883 --> 00:00:26.643
Which part of those contents was needed?

7
00:00:27.523 --> 00:00:29.663
The laboratory fractionated them.

8
00:00:30.183 --> 00:00:33.683
That narrowed the requirement to one small protein.

9
00:00:34.763 --> 00:00:40.523
Then came a test. An antibody removed that protein, and the reaction stopped.

10
00:00:41.367 --> 00:00:47.107
Then the laboratory added the pure protein back, and the reaction was restored.

11
00:00:47.987 --> 00:00:50.187
The protein was cytochrome c.

12
00:00:50.787 --> 00:00:54.787
It is the small protein that carries electrons to complex IV.

13
00:00:55.727 --> 00:00:58.927
Once it leaves the mitochondrion, it has a second job.

14
00:00:59.767 --> 00:01:06.187
The next year, the laboratory purified its partner, APAF-1, a relative of the

15
00:01:06.187 --> 00:01:15.307
worm's CED-4. Cytochrome c and dATP make seven copies of APAF-1 assemble into a

16
00:01:15.307 --> 00:01:16.807
wheel, the apoptosome.

17
00:01:17.507 --> 00:01:23.227
It gathers caspase-9, as the death-inducing signaling complex gathers caspase-8.

18
00:01:23.227 --> 00:01:27.167
Take cytochrome c away, and the reaction stops.

19
00:01:27.887 --> 00:01:29.447
Give it back, and it is restored.
